{% card "Programming Languages" %}
  <p>
    My PhD is on programming languages, so I feel confident coding in any language. My primary languages (2020‑2024) are Go and Python. I have plenty of experience in functional languages, including implementing new ones. Below is my experience with some languages; <span class="star-inline">{% icon "star" %}</span> indicates my current (2024) confidence level.
  </p>
  <ul class="ratings">
    <li><span class="what">Go</span>{% stars 5 %}<span class="note">My day‑to‑day at Canonical involves working on <a href="https://github.com/juju/juju">Juju</a> and the <a href="https://github.com/juju/terraform-provider-juju">Terraform Juju Provider</a>, both written in Go. Experienced in fault‑tolerance practices, interface design, channels and goroutines, back pressure and more.</span></li>
    <li><span class="what">C/C++</span>{% stars 4 %}<span class="note">Compilers in my <a href="{{ site.links.project_lang }}">Around the World in 26 Languages</a> project are all in C++. Wrote a garbage collector for my <a href="{{ site.links.project_rax }}">Rax</a> compiler (see Projects). <a href="https://github.com/pycket/pycket/blob/master/pycket/rktio/rktio.h">Racket IO FFI layer on Pycket</a> is all in C. Implemented a scheduler and a file system for Minix on a BeagleBoard. Did some graphics and shader programming with OpenGL.</span></li>
